[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r2
diagnosis_text:
The failure is due to an OSPF area-type mismatch on the ABR (R2), not a daemon crash. R2’s OSPF process is running (systemctl shows frr.service active/running), so there’s no evidence of an ospfd crash. On the R2–R10 link (21.1.1.0/8 on r2 eth2 and r10 eth0), OSPF Hellos show an options mismatch, which prevents adjacency formation. R2’s log reports: “Hello:RECV my options: 2, his options 0” from 110.1.4.1 (R10), while R10’s log reports the inverse for 23.1.1.1 (R2). This is the classic E-bit mismatch caused by one side treating the area as stub and the other not.

Localization and mechanism: R10’s interface shows “Area 0.0.0.4 [Stub]” on eth0 with no neighbors, while R2’s eth2 shows “Area 0.0.0.4” (no [Stub]) and also no neighbors. R2’s running config lacks “area 4 stub” under router ospf, while R10 explicitly configures “area 4 stub.” Because all routers in a stub area must agree on the stub flag, the Hello options differ and the adjacency never forms; consequently, R10 loses the ABR and the default route to the backbone.

Root cause: R2’s OSPF configuration is missing the “area 4 stub” statement after a recent change.

Corrective fix: On R2, add “area 4 stub” under router ospf (leaving networks unchanged), then clear the OSPF process or let Hellos re-negotiate. The adjacency on 21.1.1.0/8 will form and R10 will receive the default route for backbone reachability. No daemon restart is required. 
=== END ===